From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 18:45:03 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Resolve CSS into serialized inline SVG

Icon systems color glyphs with `svg { fill: currentcolor }`; pure CSS
that usvg never sees, since the serialized element only carries
attributes; sprite paths without fill attrs then default to black. With
the servo stylo build now codegen-ing `fill`, stamp the computed value
on the serialized root (skipped when the root already has an author fill
attr) so bare paths inherit it like CSS would.

Both the fill and color stamps now serialize via sRGB-legacy
rgb()/rgba(): svgtypes rejects modern serializations (oklch(),
color(srgb ...)), which design-token systems produce, and it matches
"currentColor" case-sensitively; so that keyword is emitted verbatim.

SVG2 render.html#OverflowAndClipProperties: the UA style sheet sets
overflow:hidden on svg elements. Without the rule computed overflow is
the initial visible, so blitz-paint's overflow-honouring replaced-svg
clip ("Clip replaced-svg painting to the element's content box" in
blitz-paint) would never engage; with it, an author overflow:visible
still wins and re-enables painting outside the viewport. `*|svg` because
this sheet's default @namespace is XHTML.

Inner SVG elements never cascade (they have no boxes) so their
presentation attributes reached usvg verbatim. svgtypes then rejected
anything CSS-era: `fill="var(--token)"` silently reverted to black,
`stroke="var(--token)"` dropped the stroke entirely (no gridlines), and
`font-size="0.6875rem"` was read as the bare number 0.6875. Every
charting library on the planet writes exactly these.

Run those values through the real cascade with the <svg> element as
parent style, which resolves custom properties, color-mix(),
light-dark() and CSS units alike, then stamp the computed result back
into the serialized attribute.

The property matters, so the resolution is property-aware:

- fill/stroke go through the <paint> grammar, not `color`. A var() that
  expands to `none` or `url(#grad)` is invalid for `color` and would
  compute to the inherited colour, stamping a solid fill where the
  author asked for no fill or a gradient. `stroke` is gecko-only in this
  build, so it borrows `fill`'s grammar; both parse as <paint>.
- font-size keys off the unit, not a function call: `0.6875rem` contains
  neither var() nor a colour function, and svgtypes happily reads it as
  0.6875 user units.
- Paint-server refs come back as `url(#id)`: the computed url is
  absolute and svgtypes wants the fragment, which is also what usvg
  resolves against the ids in this same serialized subtree.

Values usvg already understands, plus `none` and `currentColor`, are
left untouched, and an unparseable value bails rather than inheriting
something wrong.

usvg only sees the serialized snapshot, so host-document CSS on svg text
(font: 1px/1 monospace on an inline <svg><text>) never reached it; text
rendered at usvg's defaults regardless of the cascade (WPT
viewBox-scaling-text-001: 16px glyphs where 1px-scaled-100x was
expected, on both test and ref). Stamp computed font-size/-family/
-weight/-style as presentation attributes on text/tspan/textPath at
serialization time.

So attr-styled svg text stays correct under the stamps, the SVG
font-size presentation attribute now feeds the cascade as a pres-hint
(user units = px), making the computed value authoritative for both
sources. Verbatim serialization (sprite defs) is left unstamped: those
subtrees re-parent under a different inheritance context.
